('CIT_SAINT_GEORGE_GOLDEN','ENT_SAINT_GEORGE','SRC_GOLDEN_LEGEND','The Golden Legend (Caxton)','The Golden Legend (Caxton)','Thus the blessed George was high in de- Digitized by vjOOQIC 126 THE GOLDEN LEGEND The Life spising low things, and therefore he had verdure of S. in himself, he was attemperate by discretion, and ®eor®e therefore he had wine of gladness, and within he was plane of humility, and thereby put he forth wheat of good works. Or George may be said of gerar, that is holy, and of gyon, that is a wrestler, that is an holy wrestler, for he wrestled with the dragon.','William Caxton',1483,'https://archive.org/details/TheGoldenLegendV1',NULL,'primary-verbatim',NULL,true,'OCR scan of Caxtons Golden Legend, located by name; verify the saint.',DATE '2026-06-18','name-anchored (note-keyword scored) + substring gate; locus per attestation',1),
('CIT_SAINT_GILES_GOLDEN','ENT_SAINT_GILES','SRC_GOLDEN_LEGEND','The Golden Legend (Caxton)','The Golden Legend (Caxton)','Digitized by Google 92 THE GOLDEN LEGEND The Life of a Giles Of S. Giles. S. Giles was born in Athens, and was of noble lineage and royal kindred. And in his childhood he was informed in holy lettrure. And on a day as he went to the church, he found a sick man which lay all sick in the way and demanded alms of S. Giles, which gave him his coat. And as soon as he clad him withal he received full and entire health.','William Caxton',1483,'https://archive.org/details/TheGoldenLegendV1',NULL,'primary-verbatim',NULL,true,'OCR scan of Caxtons Golden Legend, located by name; verify the saint.',DATE '2026-06-18','name-anchored (note-keyword scored) + substring gate; locus per attestation',1),
('CIT_SAINT_JAMES_GREATER_GOLDEN','ENT_SAINT_JAMES_GREATER','SRC_GOLDEN_LEGEND','The Golden Legend (Caxton)','The Golden Legend (Caxton)','he called James and John, which were in another Aodrew ship, and they followed him, and after went into their proper places. After this he called them from their fishing, and said : Come, follow me, I shall make you fishers of men. Then they left their ships and nets, and followed him, and sifter this they abode with him, and went no more to their own houses.','William Caxton',1483,'https://archive.org/details/TheGoldenLegendV1',NULL,'primary-verbatim',NULL,true,'OCR scan of Caxtons Golden Legend, located by name; verify the saint.',DATE '2026-06-18','name-anchored (note-keyword scored) + substring gate; locus per attestation',1),
('CIT_SAINT_NICHOLAS_GOLDEN','ENT_SAINT_NICHOLAS','SRC_GOLDEN_LEGEND','The Golden Legend (Caxton)','The Golden Legend (Caxton)','yoice that said : I have gotten Nicholas by thy prayers whom I had lost. A young Christian man said to S. Andrew : My mother saw that I was fair, and required me for to have to do sin with her; and when I would not consent to her in no manner, she went to the judge, and would return and lay to me the sin of so great a felony.','William Caxton',1483,'https://archive.org/details/TheGoldenLegendV1',NULL,'primary-verbatim',NULL,true,'OCR scan of Caxtons Golden Legend, located by name; verify the saint.',DATE '2026-06-18','name-anchored (note-keyword scored) + substring gate; locus per attestation',1)
